  "harnessEpoch": 7,
  "youtubeUrl": "https://youtu.be/TwgBJ8gM1F8",
  "annotations": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarding both Lava Darts loses significant value. Lava Dart has flashback by sacrificing a Mountain, meaning each copy in the graveyard represents a free spell that triggers prowess/Slickshot Show-Off and can deal 1 damage. However, having two in the graveyard is partially redundant since you can only flash back one at a time (each requires sacrificing a Mountain). Keeping one Lava Dart in hand to cast normally (triggering prowess) and discarding one to the graveyard for flashback later would have been better. The other discard should be something like Violent Urge (situational without delirium) or one of the duplicate Preordains.",
      "actionTaken": "Discarded both copies of Lava Dart.",
      "betterLine": "Discard one Lava Dart (for graveyard flashback value) and one Violent Urge (weak without delirium and situational). Keep the other Lava Dart in hand to cast normally, triggering prowess and Slickshot abilities.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 24,
      "player": "MiniMx Analyst",
      "decisionIndex": 13
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Playing Flooded Strand onto the battlefield means it gets locked under Blood Moon (which can be cast this turn) as just a Mountain, wasting its unique ability to fetch Islands/Plains. The Arid Mesa in hand similarly becomes dead under Blood Moon, but Flooded Strand's Island-fetching ability is more unique and valuable to preserve in hand for when Blood Moon might be removed.",
      "actionTaken": "Played Flooded Strand as the land drop",
      "betterLine": "Play Arid Mesa as the land drop, keeping Flooded Strand in hand for future flexibility after Blood Moon potentially leaves play",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 25,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 15
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The player has Blood Moon in hand and plans to cast it this turn (it costs 2R). They already have Arena of Glory (which can produce R or RR if exerted) and are fetching with Arid Mesa. With Blood Moon in hand, fetching a basic Plains would be better because once Blood Moon resolves, all nonbasic lands become Mountains anyway - so Sacred Foundry's dual typing is irrelevant. Meanwhile, fetching Sacred Foundry costs 2 life (on top of the 1 life from Arid Mesa) for no benefit since Blood Moon will make it just a Mountain anyway. A basic Plains would save 2 life and still function as a Mountain under Blood Moon. However, fetching Sacred Foundry does let them cast Blood Moon this turn (Arena of Glory for R + Sacred Foundry for R + either source for the third R via exerting Arena), whereas a basic Plains only produces W without Blood Moon. Actually, let me reconsider: they need {2}{R} for Blood Moon. Arena of Glory taps for {R}, and if they fetch Sacred Foundry (untapped for 2 life), it taps for {R}. That's only 2 mana - not enough for Blood Moon this turn with just 2 lands. They could exert Arena of Glory to get {R}{R} (costs {R} to activate, so net +{R}{R} from Arena) plus Sacred Foundry for {R} = {R}{R}{R}, but exerting costs {R} which they don't have before tapping. Wait - exert cost is {R}, {T}: Add {R}{R}. So they need {R} to activate the exert ability, which they don't have from another source. So normal tap of Arena = {R}, Sacred Foundry = {R}, that's only 2 mana total. They can't cast Blood Moon this turn regardless. Given they can't cast Blood Moon this turn anyway, fetching a basic Plains would preserve the option of casting Guide of Souls ({W}) next turn while saving 2 life, and Blood Moon could come down turn 4. Sacred Foundry is slightly worse since it costs 2 extra life for marginal benefit.",
      "actionTaken": "Fetched Sacred Foundry with Arid Mesa, paying 2 additional life (1 from fetch + 2 from shock = 3 total life)",
      "betterLine": "Fetch a basic Plains to save 2 life. Blood Moon can't be cast this turn regardless (only 2 mana available), and under a future Blood Moon the Plains becomes a Mountain anyway. Plains also enables casting Guide of Souls for {W} on a future turn before Blood Moon resolves.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 27,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 19
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "With Blood Moon in hand and 3 mana available (Arena of Glory, Arid Mesa, Sacred Foundry), casting Blood Moon on turn 3 would be a strong tempo play that could lock the opponent out of non-red mana. The opponent likely has nonbasic lands that would become Mountains. Guide of Souls is a 1-drop that could be cast on a later turn alongside other spells, whereas Blood Moon is most impactful when cast early. Additionally, casting Guide of Souls before combat when holding no other creatures to trigger it immediately provides no value this turn, and it's vulnerable to the opponent's burn spells (Lava Dart flashback is available from the graveyard).",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Guide of Souls for W",
      "betterLine": "Cast Blood Moon for 2R using Arena of Glory, Arid Mesa (crack for a Mountain first if needed), and Sacred Foundry. Blood Moon would turn opponent's nonbasic lands into Mountains and could be backbreaking. Guide of Souls could be deployed later alongside creatures for value.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 29,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 21
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Casting Galvanic Discharge during combat when the opponent has no creatures or planeswalkers on the battlefield wastes a premium removal spell. The energy counters gained are minimal value compared to having the removal available for future threats like Monastery Swiftspear or Slickshot Show-Off.",
      "actionTaken": "Chose to cast Galvanic Discharge with no meaningful opposing target, likely targeting own Guide of Souls or wasting the spell.",
      "betterLine": "Skip casting spells and just attack with Guide of Souls. Save Galvanic Discharge for when the opponent deploys a creature threat. Could also crack Arid Mesa to fix mana for Blood Moon next turn if needed.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 32,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 22
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarding Monastery Swiftspear (a key 1-mana threat with haste and prowess) instead of a redundant cantrip. In an Izzet Prowess deck, threats are more valuable than the 4th cantrip. Having both Swiftspear and Slickshot Show-Off provides more threat diversity and the ability to deploy multiple threats.",
      "actionTaken": "Discarded Monastery Swiftspear",
      "betterLine": "Discard a second copy of Preordain or Expressive Iteration, keeping the cheap threat that pairs well with the spell-heavy hand.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 39,
      "player": "MiniMx Analyst",
      "decisionIndex": 26
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "GLM47 Coach cast Galvanic Discharge targeting their OWN Guide of Souls, destroying one of their best creatures for no reason. Guide of Souls is a key piece of their strategy, providing life gain, energy counters, and the ability to buff attacking creatures. Using a removal spell on your own creature is a massive waste of resources - losing both the creature and the removal spell.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Galvanic Discharge targeting their own Guide of Souls, killing it for no benefit.",
      "betterLine": "Do not cast Galvanic Discharge at all during this combat step. Save it for an opponent's creature. The opponent currently has no creatures on the battlefield, so there's nothing to target anyway - but targeting your own creature is far worse than holding the spell.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 41,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 32
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "With only one land (Fiery Islet) on the battlefield on turn 5, the player is already severely mana-screwed. Sacrificing their only land to draw a card leaves them with zero lands in play. They have 7 cards in hand including Preordain (which costs U), Expressive Iteration (which costs UR), and Slickshot Show-Off (which costs 1R) — none of which can be cast without any lands. Casting Preordain with Fiery Islet's mana would let them scry 2 and draw, helping find lands while keeping their one land in play. Sacrificing the land means even if they draw a land next turn, they'll only have one land and be even further behind.",
      "actionTaken": "Activated Fiery Islet's sacrifice ability to draw a card, destroying their only land.",
      "betterLine": "Tap Fiery Islet for U (paying 1 life) and cast Preordain to scry 2 and draw a card, digging for lands while keeping Fiery Islet in play for future turns.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 47,
      "player": "MiniMx Analyst",
      "decisionIndex": 37
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "With only one land (Fiery Islet) and 8 cards in hand, MiniMx Analyst should cast Preordain first to dig for a land drop. Casting Monastery Swiftspear before Preordain means the Swiftspear won't get a prowess trigger from Preordain if cast afterward (well, it will get prowess from subsequent spells, but that's not the issue). The real problem is sequencing: Preordain lets you scry 2 and draw, potentially finding a second land to play this turn. If you cast Swiftspear first, you've used your only mana and can't cast Preordain. Actually wait - Fiery Islet only produces one mana, so they can only cast one spell this turn regardless. But Preordain would help find a land for next turn and set up future draws, while Swiftspear with no second land means very limited ability to chain spells and leverage prowess. However, having Swiftspear on board means next turn you can cast multiple cheap spells with prowess triggers. The bigger issue is that with only 1 land and needing to find more lands desperately, Preordain is likely better here to smooth draws. Swiftspear without mana to back it up is vulnerable and not impactful.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Monastery Swiftspear using the only available mana from Fiery Islet.",
      "betterLine": "Cast Preordain to scry 2 and draw a card, digging for a crucial second land drop. With only 1 land on turn 6 (effectively turn 3 for MiniMx), finding lands is critical. Swiftspear can be deployed next turn alongside spells once more mana is available.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 55,
      "player": "MiniMx Analyst",
      "decisionIndex": 44
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "GLM47 Coach played Plains instead of considering deploying Blood Moon, which would be devastating against MiniMx Analyst's nonbasic land base (Fiery Islet becomes a Mountain). More importantly, playing Plains before deciding on the turn's plan is suboptimal sequencing. With 3 lands already in play (Arena of Glory, Arid Mesa, Sacred Foundry) and Plains as the 4th, the coach has access to 4 mana. However, the key issue is that playing Plains uses the land drop when Arid Mesa could have been cracked first to find a land, preserving the Plains in hand for a future turn. Additionally, casting Blood Moon (2R) was available this turn using Sacred Foundry + Arena of Glory + cracking Arid Mesa for a Mountain, which would turn the opponent's Fiery Islet into a basic Mountain and significantly limit their mana options. Playing Plains doesn't advance the Blood Moon plan as efficiently - though Blood Moon is still castable with Sacred Foundry (R) + Arena of Glory (R, exerted for RR) = RRR, which covers 2R. But playing Plains first and then Blood Moon means the Plains becomes a Mountain under Blood Moon. The better line was likely to cast Blood Moon this turn using existing mana sources before playing the land drop, preserving Plains for after Blood Moon resolves (where it would remain a Plains as a basic land).",
      "actionTaken": "Played Plains as land drop for turn 6",
      "betterLine": "Cast Blood Moon using Sacred Foundry + Arena of Glory (exert for RR) = 3 red mana, then play Plains afterward. This sequences Blood Moon before the land drop, and the Plains (being basic) would be unaffected by Blood Moon. Playing Plains first wastes potential since under Blood Moon it still works as basic, but the key is that deploying Blood Moon a turn earlier with this sequencing is slightly more efficient.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 63,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 48
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Blood Moon is strong here since the opponent only has Fiery Islet (which becomes a Mountain), but GLM47 Coach has 4 lands including Arid Mesa which will also become a Mountain under Blood Moon. The issue is that casting Blood Moon before cracking Arid Mesa means Arid Mesa becomes a Mountain and can no longer be sacrificed to fetch. GLM47 Coach should have cracked Arid Mesa first to get a basic land (likely a Plains for white mana access), then cast Blood Moon. With Blood Moon in play, the Sacred Foundry and Arena of Glory both become Mountains, so GLM47 Coach loses access to white mana entirely unless they fetch a Plains first. This locks out Guide of Souls, Voice of Victory, and other white cards in hand.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Blood Moon with Arid Mesa still on the battlefield unfetched.",
      "betterLine": "Activate Arid Mesa to fetch a Plains (paying 1 life), then cast Blood Moon. This preserves white mana access for Guide of Souls and Voice of Victory while still punishing the opponent's mana base.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 65,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 49
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Ragavan is a must-kill threat that will generate Treasure tokens and card advantage every combat. Under Blood Moon, MiniMx's Fiery Islet only produces red mana anyway, and most of their hand (Expressive Iteration, Preordain) requires blue mana they can't produce. Sacrificing the Mountain-ified Fiery Islet to flashback Lava Dart kills Ragavan at relatively low cost given the board state.",
      "actionTaken": "Passed without using Lava Dart flashback, leaving Ragavan alive to attack and generate value.",
      "betterLine": "Flashback Lava Dart by sacrificing Fiery Islet to kill Ragavan. Under Blood Moon, the land was only producing red mana, and most hand cards require blue mana that's unavailable anyway. Stopping Ragavan's value generation is worth the land sacrifice.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 67,
      "player": "MiniMx Analyst",
      "decisionIndex": 52
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "With delirium active (artifact, instant, creature, sorcery in graveyard), Violent Urge on the attacking Monastery Swiftspear would give +1/+0, first strike, AND double strike. Combined with prowess (+1/+1), Swiftspear would be a 3/3 double striker dealing 6 damage instead of just 1. Against an opponent at 16 life with Blood Moon constraining both players' mana, 5 extra damage is enormous.",
      "actionTaken": "Passed priority during combat without casting any spells, dealing only 1 damage with Monastery Swiftspear.",
      "betterLine": "Cast Violent Urge targeting Monastery Swiftspear for {R} from Fiery Islet (Mountain under Blood Moon). With prowess trigger and delirium-enabled double strike, Swiftspear becomes a 3/3 double striker dealing 6 combat damage, putting opponent to 10.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 73,
      "player": "MiniMx Analyst",
      "decisionIndex": 56
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Deployed Dragon's Rage Channeler instead of using Unholy Heat to kill Ragavan while delirium is active (4 card types in graveyard: artifact, instant, creature, sorcery). Ragavan on an untap will threaten combat damage generating treasure and card advantage. DRC can block Ragavan, but opponent has resources to potentially remove it.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Dragon's Rage Channeler, tapping out the only available land",
      "betterLine": "Cast Unholy Heat targeting Ragavan (deals 6 with delirium active) to remove the high-value threat, then deploy DRC on a future turn when more mana is available",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 76,
      "player": "MiniMx Analyst",
      "decisionIndex": 57
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Casting Violent Urge on Monastery Swiftspear in precombat main phase with no combat context wastes the spell. Violent Urge gives +1/+0 and first strike (or double strike with delirium) until end of turn - this is a combat trick that should be cast during combat to maximize damage or in response to removal. Casting it now triggers prowess on Swiftspear and DRC's surveil, but the card is much more valuable as a combat trick. Additionally, Blood Moon is on the battlefield turning all nonbasic lands into Mountains, meaning Fiery Islet is a Mountain. MiniMx has delirium check: graveyard has Mishra's Bauble (artifact), Lava Dart (instant), Monastery Swiftspear (creature), Preordain (sorcery) - that's 4 types, so delirium is active and Violent Urge would grant double strike. This makes it even more valuable to save for combat when you can use it on an attacking creature for maximum damage. However, the bigger issue is that casting it precombat means the opponent sees it and can make blocking decisions accordingly, whereas casting during combat after blocks would be much stronger.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Violent Urge targeting Monastery Swiftspear during precombat main phase",
      "betterLine": "Save Violent Urge for the combat phase (during declare attackers or after blockers are declared) to use it as a surprise combat trick with double strike from delirium. In precombat main, could play Bloodstained Mire first, or cast Lava Dart to kill Ragavan (which is a significant threat that generates card advantage), then attack.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 81,
      "player": "MiniMx Analyst",
      "decisionIndex": 61
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "GLM47 Coach declined to attack with Ragavan, Nimble Pilferer when both of the opponent's creatures (Monastery Swiftspear and Dragon's Rage Channeler) were tapped. This means there were zero blockers available, making the attack completely free. Attacking would deal 2 damage, create a Treasure token (useful for casting the Seasoned Pyromancer or Voice of Victory in hand), and exile the top card of the opponent's library for potential use.",
      "actionTaken": "Did not attack with Ragavan despite no blockers being available.",
      "betterLine": "Attack with Ragavan for free 2 damage, a Treasure token, and a potential exiled card to cast. At 3 life in a losing position, every resource matters.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 92,
      "player": "GLM47 Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 70
    }
  ],
